{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  "cacheDir": ".turbo/cache",
  "globalDependencies": [
    "pnpm-lock.yaml",
    "pnpm-workspace.yaml",
    "tsconfig.json",
    // The config BOTH slow tiers actually run under (`vitest run -c
    // ../../vitest.slow.config.ts`). `check:integration` used to name a
    // `vitest.integration.config.ts` that has never existed, so editing the
    // real one changed nothing and turbo replayed the previous run. Same class
    // of miss as the root tsconfig above: a root file a task depends on has to
    // be hashed, and `inputs` globs resolve relative to the PACKAGE.
    "vitest.slow.config.ts",
    // The platform schema, read by six suites — including
    // `platform-schema.test.ts`, which is in the UNIT tier and whose whole job
    // is to assert every `aai_platform.<table>` the source queries is declared
    // by a migration. Third instance of the rule two entries up: a repo-root
    // directory is covered by no package's `inputs`, so editing a migration
    // could not invalidate the cache of the test that guards migrations, in
    // either tier. Verified the documented way — capture `turbo run test
    // --filter aai-server --dry=json`'s hash, touch a migration, capture
    // again; before this entry `aai-server#test` was byte-identical.
    "supabase/migrations/**",
    // `aai-server`'s vitest `globalSetup` (`vitest.config.ts` names it by
    // repo-relative path), plus `aai-studio-server`'s `predev` and
    // `aai-server`'s `predeploy:modal`. Fourth instance of the rule above and
    // the same fix: `inputs` globs resolve relative to the PACKAGE, so a
    // repo-root file a task depends on belongs here. Verified the documented
    // way — capture `turbo run test --filter ./packages/aai-server --dry=json`'s
    // hash, touch this script, capture again; before this entry the hash was
    // byte-identical, so a change to the harness verify logic replayed a cached
    // green run.
    "scripts/ensure-guest-harness.mjs",
    // Every project's vitest `setupFiles` entry, named by `sharedSetupFiles` in
    // `vitest.shared.ts` as an absolute path. Fifth instance of the rule above:
    // `inputs` globs resolve relative to the PACKAGE, so a repo-root file every
    // test task loads is hashed by none of them — and this one decides whether a
    // suite FAILS on a listener leak, so a cached green run would replay past a
    // change to the gate itself.
    "scripts/fail-on-process-warning.mjs"
  ],
  "globalPassThroughEnv": [
    // Ambient machine config, same class as the proxy/CA vars below. Stripped
    // by strict env mode, `os.tmpdir()` falls back to `/tmp` — shared, not
    // per-user, and not cleaned between runs, so anything a task writes there
    // collides with every other user and with its own previous runs. It also
    // changes behaviour: `npm prefix` walks up for the nearest `package.json`
    // or `node_modules`, and a stray `/tmp/node_modules` (easy to acquire from
    // one `npm install` in the wrong shell) makes it report `/tmp` instead of
    // the directory npm was given.
    "TMPDIR",
    "TMP",
    "TEMP",
    // Read by `vitest.shared.ts` to bound `maxWorkers` per run, because turbo's
    // concurrency bounds TASKS and says nothing about the workers each one
    // spawns — only the product is what the machine feels. See `workerBudget`
    // there for the 8-processes-on-4-cores measurement and the push it cost.
    //
    // Unlike every other entry in this list, this one is NOT load-bearing for
    // passthrough, and saying so is the point: `TURBO_*` is turbo's own
    // namespace and reaches tasks whatever strict env mode does to everything
    // else. Measured — exported and undeclared, the task still read it. It is
    // here so the declaration matches what the code reads, which is what
    // `noUndeclaredEnvVars` checks; a warning nothing fails on is how this repo
    // gets rules that check nothing. `globalPassThroughEnv` rather than a task
    // `env` because it changes how many processes run the tests, never what they
    // conclude, so it must not fragment the cache across core counts.
    "TURBO_CONCURRENCY",
    // Read by `scripts/dev-server.mjs`, which no turbo task runs — declared for
    // the same reason `TURBO_CONCURRENCY` above is, so the declaration matches
    // what the code reads and `noUndeclaredEnvVars` has something true to check.
    // `globalPassThroughEnv` rather than a task `env` because both are ambient
    // machine settings for a developer's own server (which port is free, which
    // tunnel is in front of it) and neither changes what any cached task
    // concludes.
    "PORT",
    "AAI_PUBLIC_ORIGIN",
    "HTTP_PROXY",
    "HTTPS_PROXY",
    "NO_PROXY",
    "http_proxy",
    "https_proxy",
    "no_proxy",
    "npm_config_proxy",
    "npm_config_https_proxy",
    "npm_config_noproxy",
    "NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS",
    "SSL_CERT_FILE",
    "SSL_CERT_DIR",
    "ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY",
    "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY",
    "GUEST_HARNESS_PATH",
    "TURBO_HASH",
    // Ambient CI marker, not a behaviour switch: the fs gates (`check:invariants`,
    // `check:konsistent`) read it only to decide whether to also emit
    // `::error file=…` annotations so violations land inline on the PR diff.
    // The pass/fail is identical either way, which is exactly why this belongs
    // here and not in a task's `env` — putting it there would fragment every
    // task's cache between local and CI runs for a formatting difference.
    "GITHUB_ACTIONS",
    // The local/deployed Supabase stack: a developer's own `supabase start`
    // values, read by `scripts/dev-server.mjs` and by `aai-server`'s runtime
    // config. Ambient machine config in the same sense as the proxy vars above
    // — no cached task's OUTPUT depends on them (every spec that reads one
    // `vi.stubEnv`s it), so putting them in a task's `env` would fragment that
    // task's cache between a developer with a local stack and one without, for
    // no difference in what the task produces. Declared because biome's turbo
    // domain rule `noUndeclaredEnvVars` fails `lint:scripts` otherwise, which
    // is the gate reporting a real gap: an undeclared variable is stripped
    // SILENTLY by strict env mode.
    "AAI_LOCAL_DEV",
    "SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN",
    "SUPABASE_DB_URL",
    "SUPABASE_MANAGEMENT_URL",
    "SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF",
    "SUPABASE_STORAGE_BUCKET"
  ],
  "tasks": {
    // `$TURBO_DEFAULT$` — every git-tracked file in the package — rather than a
    // hand-kept list of extensions. The list missed the Vite inputs of the two
    // packages that have them: `aai-ui#build` did not hash `index.html`,
    // `styles.css` or `public/**`, and `aai-studio-client#build` missed those
    // plus `src/assets/*.svg` and `src/fonts/*.woff2` — so a Tailwind- or
    // HTML-only change was a FULL TURBO replay of the previous bundle, and CI's
    // setup job handed that stale dist to every downstream job. An extension
    // list can only ever be right about the file types that exist today.
    //
    // Markdown is excluded because no build reads it and CHANGELOG.md is
    // rewritten by every changesets release. `aai-cli` deliberately does NOT
    // inherit that exclusion — it bundles the templates and the scaffold, whose
    // markdown (scaffold/CLAUDE.md above all) is shipped product.
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "^build"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "!**/*.test.ts",
        "!**/*.test.tsx",
        "!**/*.test-d.ts",
        "!**/*.md"
      ],
      "outputs": [
        "dist/**"
      ]
    },
    // Tests read more than TypeScript: JSON fixtures (`aai/host/fixtures/`,
    // `aai-ui/fixtures/`, `aai/sdk/compat-fixtures/`) and snapshots
    // (`__snapshots__/*.snap`) were all unhashed, so an edited fixture — or an
    // obsolete snapshot, which `update: "none"` exists to turn into a FAILURE —
    // replayed green. `$TURBO_DEFAULT$` covers whatever a fixture is next
    // written in. Markdown is excluded here as it is for build; the one suite
    // that really does read markdown (aai-templates) re-includes it, along with
    // the other repo-root files it reads, in its own turbo.json.
    //
    // VITEST_POOL is DECLARED, not passed through: turbo runs in strict env
    // mode, so an undeclared variable never reaches the task at all. Declaring
    // it in `env` also puts it in the hash, so a forks run and a threads run
    // cannot share a cache entry.
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "^build"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "!**/*.md",
        "../../vitest.shared.ts"
      ],
      "env": [
        "CI",
        "VITEST_POOL"
      ]
    },
    "test:coverage": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "^build"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "!**/*.md",
        "../../vitest.shared.ts"
      ],
      "outputs": [
        "coverage/**"
      ],
      "env": [
        "CI",
        "VITEST_POOL"
      ]
    },
    // `dependsOn: ["build"]` is SCHEDULING, not a requirement: every package
    // type-checks with no `dist` present anywhere, because `@dev/source`
    // resolves cross-package imports to TypeScript source. It is kept because
    // dropping it is slower, not faster — tsc and tsdown then run concurrently
    // and fight for cores (measured on 4 cores: `turbo run build typecheck
    // --force`, 21.2s with the dependency, 25.7s without).
    "typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "build"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "**/*.ts",
        "**/*.tsx",
        "tsconfig*.json"
      ]
    },
    // `biome check .` lints JSON and CSS as well as TS/TSX — a formatting error
    // injected into `packages/aai/typedoc.json` is reported by biome but left
    // the lint hash untouched while this listed only `**/*.ts`/`**/*.tsx`.
    // `$TURBO_DEFAULT$` keeps this correct as biome grows languages.
    "lint": {
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "../../biome.json"
      ]
    },
    "check:publint": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "build"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "dist/**",
        "package.json"
      ]
    },
    "check:attw": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "build"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "dist/**",
        "package.json"
      ]
    },
    // The integration tier is now in-memory ONLY — multiple modules, no
    // filesystem, no subprocess, no port, no database (see the profile docs in
    // `vitest.slow.config.ts`). Everything that needs a real resource is
    // `check:scenario` below, which is where the env declarations moved with it.
    //
    // VITEST_PROFILE/VITEST_INCLUDE are declared even though each package's script
    // sets them inline: a variable that selects what a task DOES belongs in the
    // hash, so an override from the command line cannot share a cache entry with
    // the default run. That is the rule `AAI_TEST_PM` and `VITEST_POOL` are in
    // `env` for.
    "check:integration": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "build"
      ],
      "env": [
        "VITEST_PROFILE",
        "VITEST_INCLUDE",
        "VITEST_SETUP",
        "VITEST_POOL",
        // `pipeline-reference.integration.test.ts` SKIPS without a reference
        // stack and this turns that skip into a hard failure. Same shape and
        // same reason as `AAI_REQUIRE_PG` / `AAI_REQUIRE_STACK` on
        // `check:scenario` below: strict env mode strips an undeclared variable
        // before the task starts, so the enforcement would be silently inert —
        // which is the exact failure it exists to prevent. In `env` rather than
        // `globalPassThroughEnv` because it selects what the task DOES: a
        // required run and a skippable one must not share a cache entry.
        "AAI_REQUIRE_REFERENCE_STACK"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "!**/*.md",
        "../../vitest.shared.ts"
      ]
    },
    // A real subprocess, a real HTTP/WebSocket port, a real bundler, or a real
    // Postgres. Split out of `check:integration` because one config could not
    // serve both populations: `pipeline-fuzz` (pure memory) and
    // `platform-schema` (needs a database) shared one timeout, one retry policy
    // and one queue, and neither was configured for its own failure mode.
    //
    // AAI_REQUIRE_PG rides beside the URL for the reason the AAI_TEST_PM note
    // below spells out: strict env mode strips an undeclared variable before
    // the task starts, so CI's "a skip is a failure" enforcement would be
    // silently inert — the failure mode it exists to prevent, one level up.
    // In `env` rather than `globalPassThroughEnv` because it changes what the
    // task DOES: a required run and a skippable one must not share a cache
    // entry.
    "check:scenario": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "build"
      ],
      "env": [
        "AAI_TEST_PG_URL",
        "AAI_REQUIRE_PG",
        // Same shape and same reason one tier over: the microVM scenario suite
        // SKIPS without hardware virtualization or a built guest image, and this
        // turns that skip into a hard failure on a runner that is supposed to
        // provide both. Undeclared, strict env mode strips it before the task
        // starts and the enforcement is silently inert.
        "AAI_REQUIRE_MICROSANDBOX",
        // Selects the guest image the microVM backend boots — a registry-pulled
        // one instead of the local build — so it changes what the task DOES and
        // must not share a cache entry with a run that used the other.
        "GUEST_IMAGE_REGISTRY",
        // The whole local Supabase stack, resolved by `with-test-pg.mjs` out of
        // `supabase status -o env`. Declared for both of AAI_REQUIRE_PG's
        // reasons and they bite harder here: these are the ONLY arm the platform
        // stores have (a stock Postgres runs none of Vault, pg_cron or walrus),
        // so a stripped variable is not a narrower run, it is the platform tier
        // silently absent. In `env`, so a stack run and a database-only run
        // cannot share a cache entry.
        "AAI_TEST_SUPABASE_URL",
        "AAI_TEST_SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY",
        "AAI_TEST_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY",
        "AAI_REQUIRE_STACK",
        // ffmpeg for `aai/host/ffmpeg.scenario.test.ts`, the only thing that
        // checks the ARGV those helpers build against a real binary. Declared
        // for the same reason as the two above: strict env mode strips an
        // undeclared variable, so "a skip here is a failure" would be inert —
        // and this suite skips SILENTLY useful only if nobody relies on it.
        "AAI_REQUIRE_FFMPEG",
        // Deno for `aai-cli/_deno-output.scenario.test.ts`, the only thing that
        // proves an `aai build --target deno` directory boots under a real Deno.
        // Declared for the same reason as the three above — strict env mode
        // strips an undeclared variable before the task starts, so "a skip here
        // is a failure" would be silently inert, which is precisely the state
        // that suite shipped in.
        "AAI_REQUIRE_DENO",
        "AAI_FAULT_PROFILE",
        "VITEST_PROFILE",
        "VITEST_INCLUDE",
        "VITEST_SETUP",
        "VITEST_POOL"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "!**/*.md",
        "../../vitest.shared.ts"
      ]
    },
    // AAI_TEST_PM is the documented way to reproduce a user report under npm or
    // yarn (`AAI_TEST_PM=npm pnpm test:e2e`). `pnpm test:e2e` IS `turbo run
    // check:e2e`, and under strict env mode an undeclared variable is stripped
    // before the task starts — so that command silently ran pnpm. Declaring it
    // here both passes it through and makes each package manager its own cache
    // entry.
    //
    // AAI_FAULT_PROFILE is here for both halves of that same reason
    // (`AAI_FAULT_PROFILE=restart-on-boot pnpm test:e2e` would otherwise run
    // with no faults and say nothing), and the cache half matters more than
    // usual: a fault run and a clean run of the same tree MUST NOT share a cache
    // entry, or the first green clean run would serve a FULL TURBO for every
    // later fault run and the mode would test nothing.
    //
    // AAI_REQUIRE_REGISTRY is the third instance of the same pair of reasons, and
    // it is the one where a silent strip would be worst: it turns the suite's
    // "excuse a failed install as a registry-proxy flake" predicate OFF, so
    // undeclared it would leave CI's enforcement inert — a gate reporting success
    // while checking nothing, which is exactly what it exists to prevent. In
    // `env`, so a required run and a skippable one are different cache entries.
    // VITEST_POOL is the fourth instance, and it was MISSING while the three
    // above were declared — `VITEST_POOL=forks pnpm test:e2e` was stripped
    // silently, the same class the paragraph above documents. The other four
    // test tasks all declare it.
    //
    // `../../vitest.shared.ts` likewise: the e2e run reaches `sharedConfig`
    // through `vitest.slow.config.ts`, and the other four test tasks all hash
    // it. Verified the documented way — capture
    // `turbo run check:e2e --filter ./packages/aai-cli --dry=json`'s hash, touch
    // `vitest.shared.ts`, capture again; before this entry it was
    // byte-identical at `261096f60c7229f6`.
    "check:e2e": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "build"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "!**/*.md",
        "../../vitest.shared.ts"
      ],
      "env": [
        "AAI_TEST_PM",
        "AAI_FAULT_PROFILE",
        "AAI_REQUIRE_REGISTRY",
        "AAI_REQUIRE_BROWSER",
        "VITEST_POOL",
        // `aai-cli`'s `test:e2e` sets it (`VITEST_SETUP=./_test-setup.ts`), and
        // `vitest.slow.config.ts` reads it to pick `setupFiles` — the file that
        // points AAI_CONFIG_DIR away from the developer's real
        // `~/.config/aai/config.json`. Its three siblings were declared and this
        // one was not, on all four tasks sharing that config. Not a live strip
        // (the package script assigns it inline, downstream of strict env mode),
        // so this is the hash half of the rule the VITEST_PROFILE note states:
        // a variable selecting what a task DOES belongs in the hash. Found by
        // `noUndeclaredEnvVars` the first time Biome was allowed to read a
        // root-level file at all.
        "VITEST_SETUP"
      ]
    },
    // The behaviour eval tier: a live model, a live key, real tokens. NOT in
    // `pnpm check` and not in CI — see `packages/aai-evals/CLAUDE.md` for why a
    // measurably noisy instrument must not be a merge gate.
    //
    // Every variable here SELECTS WHAT THE TASK DOES, which is the rule that
    // puts it in `env` rather than `globalPassThroughEnv`: strict env mode
    // strips an undeclared one silently, which is the failure that made
    // `AAI_TEST_PM=npm pnpm test:e2e` run pnpm and say nothing. `AAI_ORIGIN` and
    // `AAI_STEP_CAP_HINT` used to sit in `globalPassThroughEnv` for want of a
    // task to own them; the first is `AAI_EVAL_ORIGIN` now, since it names this
    // tier's target rather than ambient machine config.
    //
    // **`cache: false`, which is the one place this repo's `inputs` rule does
    // not apply.** Everywhere else a task is a pure function of its inputs and
    // the fix for a replayed green run is to hash more; here two runs of the
    // SAME tree legitimately differ — that is the documented property the tier
    // exists to report (0.56 against 0.60 on identical code, 9 of 25 tasks
    // flipping). A cache hit would therefore replay a measurement rather than
    // take one, so the second `pnpm test:eval` of a variance check would print
    // FULL TURBO and the number from the first. There is consequently no input
    // set to get right, and none is declared rather than declaring one that
    // nothing reads. If this ever becomes cacheable, a package-relative
    // `$TURBO_DEFAULT$` is now enough: the starter corpus used to live OUTSIDE
    // the package (`scripts/starter-eval/expectations.mjs`, hashed by a
    // per-package `inputs` override) and is `packages/aai-evals/
    // starter-expectations.ts` today.
    "check:eval": {
      "cache": false,
      "dependsOn": [
        "build"
      ],
      "env": [
        "AAI_EVAL_CONTRACTS",
        "AAI_REQUIRE_EVAL",
        // Forces the SCRIPTED model even where a key is present — how CI runs
        // the template evals as a wiring gate. In `env` rather than
        // `globalPassThroughEnv` because it selects what the task DOES, so a
        // stub run and a live run must not share a cache entry (this task is
        // `cache: false` anyway; the rule is the rule).
        "AAI_EVAL_STUB",
        // The key the tier spends. `aai-evals` reads it through its own gate
        // (which also falls back to what `aai login` saved), but a TEMPLATE's
        // eval reads the environment and nothing else — it ships to users, so
        // it may not go looking in a developer's CLI config — and strict env
        // mode strips an undeclared variable silently, which would leave every
        // template case skipping with a key exported right there in the shell.
        "ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY",
        "AAI_EVAL_ORIGIN",
        "AAI_EVAL_REPEAT",
        "AAI_EVAL_ONLY",
        "AAI_EVAL_MIN_SCORE",
        "AAI_STEP_CAP_HINT",
        // `call-audit`'s eval decodes with a REAL ffmpeg and skips without one,
        // so it takes the same require-flag as `check:scenario` — and for the
        // same reason spelled out there: strict env mode strips an undeclared
        // variable, which would make "a skip here is a failure" inert on the one
        // leg that installs the binary. Declared on BOTH tasks because the CI
        // job that sets it now runs both.
        "AAI_REQUIRE_FFMPEG",
        "VITEST_PROFILE",
        "VITEST_INCLUDE",
        "VITEST_SETUP",
        "VITEST_POOL"
      ]
    },
    "//#check:syncpack": {
      "inputs": [
        "package.json",
        "packages/*/package.json",
        ".syncpackrc.json"
      ]
    },
    // The other half of the same tool, on the same inputs: `check:syncpack`
    // reads dependency VERSIONS, this reads manifest SHAPE (key order, and the
    // order of the conditions inside `exports`). It was installed and invoked
    // by nothing, and all eleven manifests failed it.
    //
    // `sortExports` in `.syncpackrc.json` is load-bearing rather than
    // cosmetic. Under the default list `@dev/source` is unknown and sorts
    // LAST, which puts `import` ahead of it — and condition resolution takes
    // the first match, so every dev-source path would silently resolve to
    // `dist/` and the custom condition the whole workspace is built on would
    // stop working. The config names it first.
    "//#check:format": {
      "inputs": [
        "package.json",
        "packages/*/package.json",
        ".syncpackrc.json"
      ]
    },
    // Duplicate versions of one package resolved side by side in the lockfile.
    // pnpm answers this directly and nothing asked it: the tree carried
    // rolldown 1.2.0 beside 1.2.4, two @types/node, two @oxc-project/types and
    // sixteen duplicate @rolldown/binding-* platform packages. That is bytes in
    // the guest harness bundle `artifact-size-report.mjs` budgets, and a second
    // copy of a type package is also two versions of one global type surface.
    //
    // Keyed on the lockfile, so a clean tree is a cache hit rather than a
    // resolution.
    "//#check:dedupe": {
      "inputs": [
        "pnpm-lock.yaml",
        "pnpm-workspace.yaml",
        "package.json",
        "packages/*/package.json"
      ]
    },
    "//#check:sherif": {
      "inputs": [
        "package.json",
        "packages/*/package.json",
        "pnpm-workspace.yaml"
      ]
    },
    "//#check:knip": {
      "inputs": [
        "packages/**/*.ts",
        "packages/**/*.tsx",
        "knip.json",
        "package.json",
        "packages/*/package.json"
      ]
    },
    "//#check:markdown": {
      "inputs": [
        "**/*.md"
      ]
    },
    // Everything Biome lints that lives OUTSIDE a package, which `turbo run lint`
    // structurally cannot reach: that task runs `biome check .` from each
    // PACKAGE directory, so the repo's own root-level TypeScript and `examples/`
    // were linted by nothing. Adding them to `files.includes` only PERMITS
    // biome to read them — something still has to point it at the path, which
    // is the dead-config trap this repo keeps paying for. Verified by injecting
    // a format error into `vitest.slow.config.ts`: `biome check scripts/` saw
    // 51 files and not that one, and every package run saw its own 18.
    //
    // Renamed from `//#lint:scripts` because the target is no longer only
    // `scripts/`. `*.ts` is expanded by the SHELL — biome does not expand globs
    // itself (`biome check "*.ts"` reports "No such file or directory").
    "//#lint:root": {
      "inputs": [
        "scripts/**",
        "examples/**",
        "*.ts",
        "biome.json"
      ]
    },
    // The root-level TypeScript, which no package's `typecheck` could reach:
    // each of those runs under its own package tsconfig, and the root
    // `tsconfig.json` is a base nothing points `tsc` at. See
    // `tsconfig.tools.json` for the injected-error proof.
    //
    // `packages/aai/**` and `packages/aai-ui/**` are inputs because the config
    // `paths`-maps the two published specifiers to workspace SOURCE, so this
    // task really does read them — the same rule as the root `tsconfig.json` in
    // `globalDependencies`: a file a task depends on has to be hashed by that
    // task, and a root task's globs are the only ones that can see outside a
    // package. No `dependsOn`: it resolves source, so it needs no `dist`.
    // The JAVASCRIPT half of the same job — see `tsconfig.scripts.json`'s header.
    // Same `inputs` rule as the task below: a file this reads has to be hashed
    // by it, and a root task's globs are the only ones that can see outside a
    // package. The three `packages/**` entries are there because the config
    // `paths`-maps the published specifiers to workspace SOURCE for the
    // examples, so this task really does read them.
    "//#typecheck:scripts": {
      "inputs": [
        "scripts/**/*.mjs",
        "examples/**/*.mjs",
        "tsconfig.scripts.json",
        "packages/aai/**/*.ts",
        "packages/aai-ui/**/*.ts",
        "packages/aai-runtime/**/*.ts"
      ]
    },
    "//#typecheck:tools": {
      "inputs": [
        "*.ts",
        "examples/**/*.ts",
        "tsconfig.tools.json",
        "packages/aai/**/*.ts",
        "packages/aai-ui/**/*.ts"
      ]
    },
    "docs": {
      "dependsOn": [
        "@alexkroman1/aai#build",
        "@alexkroman1/aai-ui#build"
      ],
      "inputs": [
        "typedoc.json",
        "home.md",
        "tsconfig.typedoc.json",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/aai/typedoc.json",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/aai-ui/typedoc.json"
      ],
      "outputs": [
        "dist/**"
      ]
    }
  }
}
